WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
In July 2020, I had 433,000 followers on Instagram.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.360
So your account has grown a hundredfold since Covid.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.240
Exactly.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:11.800
Which story gave your career the biggest boost?

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.039
Cristiano Ronaldo’s return to Manchester United.

00:00:14.039 --> 00:00:17.839
I remember being live on Twitch and there were 97,000 people connected,

00:00:17.839 --> 00:00:18.399
so more than a full Camp Nou.

00:00:18.399 --> 00:00:18.839
That’s insane...

00:00:18.839 --> 00:00:23.800
I remember that when I started posting transfer news on Instagram, Corriere, Italy's most read newspaper did not have an account.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.079
Does being first matter that much?

00:00:25.079 --> 00:00:27.239
Having information before others is extremely important.

00:00:27.239 --> 00:00:30.800
In the long run, though, accuracy matters a lot more to me.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.880
Today you can break the best news in the world, but tomorrow morning people have already forgotten it,

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:35.640
and they already want another one.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.479
This is Fabrizio Romano, a star of international football,

00:00:38.479 --> 00:00:40.719
even though he has never touched a ball in public.

00:00:40.719 --> 00:00:45.719
And no, it is not a paradox. Fabrizio is a journalist and has built one of the world's most unique careers,

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:49.320
becoming number one in a very specific field, the transfer market.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.640
On Instagram alone, he has tens of millions of followers

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.799
making him probably one of the most famous Italians in the world.

00:00:54.799 --> 00:00:59.679
During transfer windows, his Twitter account gets more interactions than Elon Musk's.

00:00:59.679 --> 00:01:01.759
The transfer market is a huge niche.

00:01:01.759 --> 00:01:04.719
Anyone like me, who never truly followed football, might be surprised.

00:01:04.719 --> 00:01:07.519
But people are not only interested in watching games,

00:01:07.519 --> 00:01:10.319
they also want to know the backstage of everything that happens.

00:01:10.319 --> 00:01:14.239
And above all, which players are bought and sold between clubs.

00:01:14.239 --> 00:01:15.319
That is the transfer market.

00:01:15.319 --> 00:01:19.680
When a player is about to move from one club to another after signing a contract worth millions,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.359
the first to break the news is Fabrizio Romano, who tells you how the deal is going,

00:01:23.359 --> 00:01:26.439
how many millions are involved and what the contract fine print is.

00:01:26.439 --> 00:01:29.519
To be effective, Fabrizio had to build a network of relationships

00:01:29.519 --> 00:01:34.480
with agents, players and industry insiders he talks to all day long.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.719
So one day I called Fabrizio, invited him to my office, and had him tell me his story.

00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:40.599
As usual, starting from the beginning.

00:01:40.599 --> 00:01:41.280
Here we go!

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:42.239
Where are you originally from?

00:01:42.239 --> 00:01:43.519
Naples, Posillipo.

00:01:43.519 --> 00:01:44.719
You have completely lost the accent!

00:01:44.719 --> 00:01:46.599
Yes, actually I never really had one.

00:01:46.599 --> 00:01:49.359
But yes, after 15 years here in Milan...

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:52.439
When did you become passionate about football?

00:01:52.439 --> 00:01:54.359
When did you think, "I want to do this for a living"?

00:01:54.359 --> 00:01:57.359
Look, I have loved football for as long as I can remember.

00:01:57.359 --> 00:02:02.560
My first memories, not all but many, are tied to football, from the classic street game,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.599
which feels very Naples and was truly part of my childhood,

00:02:05.599 --> 00:02:09.080
to my dad, my brother, watching matches together.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.560
There was always a huge sharing of everything about football. It really was my life.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.560
I owe everything to my dad, my brother and Naples, my city, Maradona.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.120
I imagine they were big Napoli fans, right?

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:22.199
Yes, yes, absolutely, but above all devoted Maradonians.

00:02:22.199 --> 00:02:24.879
When people ask me "Ronaldo or Messi?" for me it is always Maradona.

00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:26.360
Did you want to become a football player?

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:30.919
I never even really thought about it, because I never had even a 1% chance,

00:02:30.919 --> 00:02:35.199
because my technical ability was never even remotely close to that level.

00:02:35.199 --> 00:02:38.199
Then I was also lucky to realize quite early

00:02:38.199 --> 00:02:41.479
that the football industry could still become my future

00:02:41.479 --> 00:02:43.479
and to become passionate about it very fast.

00:02:43.479 --> 00:02:47.599
I think what made the difference in my life, in journalism and storytelling,

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:48.719
was timing.

00:02:48.719 --> 00:02:52.680
Compared to my peers from school to university,

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.879
many had doubts, which is completely normal.

00:02:55.879 --> 00:03:00.159
I was lucky enough to understand early, already in high school, what I wanted to do.

00:03:00.159 --> 00:03:02.520
So I never had a plan B, C, or D in my head.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:03.479
And what did you understand in high school?

00:03:03.479 --> 00:03:04.199
What did you want to do?

00:03:04.199 --> 00:03:07.199
Since I was not that great at football, I wanted to tell people about it.

00:03:07.199 --> 00:03:13.759
I wanted to be part of that world because it was a real obsession, in the best possible way,

00:03:13.759 --> 00:03:19.120
I spent my time playing football, sometimes even two five-a-side games, two games a day with high school friends.

00:03:19.719 --> 00:03:22.479
Two a day! We were really going at a crazy pace.

00:03:22.479 --> 00:03:27.000
We watched every match together, we played the PlayStation together, Football Manager together,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:29.240
so it was not just knowing Serie A matches,

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:33.719
but it was also about discovering young players in South America, in Norway,

00:03:33.719 --> 00:03:36.639
We were always going beyond a basic passion.

00:03:36.639 --> 00:03:40.520
So I said, "I love this world too much not to make it my life"

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.400
and I quickly understood I wanted to be part of storytelling.

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.879
I always loved chatting and reading as a child.

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:51.520
My dad was born in 1942, to a regular kid, he’s basically grandpa-aged.

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:53.840
I mean, I never really had the chance to play football with my father,

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:58.599
but he gave me a lot in terms of reading, telling stories and speaking well,

00:03:58.599 --> 00:04:01.560
and I carried that into wanting to do this job.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:07.080
So I understood it right away, and especially understood quite early that I wanted this kind of journalism,

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.479
because saying "I want to be a sports journalist" is such a broad world

00:04:10.479 --> 00:04:15.039
that you can do it through television, newspapers, radio,

00:04:15.039 --> 00:04:17.360
social media today, so there are a thousand ways to do it.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.639
I discovered the transfer market early and I was lucky to enter it as a kid.

00:04:21.639 --> 00:04:26.800
When you were in high school, there were no digital creators, maybe the very first YouTubers abroad.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:27.480
Yes, very few.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:28.399
But you did not want to do that?

00:04:28.399 --> 00:04:29.199
Not at all.

00:04:29.199 --> 00:04:31.519
At the start, it was never one of my goals.

00:04:31.519 --> 00:04:34.439
In fact, my dream at first was always more tied to newspapers.

00:04:34.439 --> 00:04:36.920
I started writing for websites at 16, football websites.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.600
Your own sites or other people's?

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:39.519
No, other people's sites.

00:04:39.519 --> 00:04:45.040
I used to send emails or even Facebook messages asking, "Can I write for you for free?"

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.240
I did not care about anything. It was just after school, to see if I could improve,

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:50.279
and understand whether I really liked it.

00:04:50.279 --> 00:04:52.079
So that is what I did. I started on the web.

00:04:52.079 --> 00:04:55.920
Then the first real experience was television, because I got into Sky.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:03.000
I finished classical high school in Naples in 2011, I was 18, born in 1993, and right after, a year later,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:06.720
I started, as I said before, to write for these general football websites

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.800
and then collaborating with Gianluca Di Marzio from Sky, who had created his own website.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.439
So I saw he was launching this site, I sent an email,

00:05:13.439 --> 00:05:16.439
actually, I first sent a Facebook message at night, then an email saying:

00:05:16.439 --> 00:05:20.480
"Look, I saw you are starting a new site. If you need someone, I am available."

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:24.319
Here too, I was very lucky to find someone who opened the doors for me

00:05:24.319 --> 00:05:26.240
and gave me the chance to say, "Let's see how it goes."

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:30.480
There were many of us. In the end it was me and a friend who still works with me, one of my best friends.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:36.639
It was the chance to realize: okay, I really like this world, with its pros and cons,

00:05:36.639 --> 00:05:39.800
the timing and everything else that still has to be considered.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:44.480
Between 2012 and 2013, I joined Sky Sport and had my first experience as a journalist,

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:48.439
obviously as a summer replacement, but it was the start of a path.

00:05:48.439 --> 00:05:50.199
How long did the Sky Sport experience last?

00:05:50.199 --> 00:05:51.040
It lasted quite a while.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:56.040
I finished during Covid, just after, between 2020 and 2021, because it had changed a lot.

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:58.839
The first thing I realized was that I no longer wanted to be in there.

00:05:58.839 --> 00:06:02.319
I never liked the office dynamics, the newsroom dynamics.

00:06:02.319 --> 00:06:03.879
I remember I used to say, "I am losing so much time here."

00:06:03.879 --> 00:06:08.199
At least that was my impression, I am not judging Sky or anyone, it was just my office feeling,

00:06:08.199 --> 00:06:09.759
"I want to meet new people."

00:06:09.759 --> 00:06:13.399
If I go to the office, I always meet the same people, even if some were my idols.

00:06:13.399 --> 00:06:17.079
I wanted to try to meet more people to do my job.

00:06:17.079 --> 00:06:20.839
As a journalist, being able to connect with as many people as possible

00:06:20.839 --> 00:06:22.279
is probably rule number one.

00:06:22.279 --> 00:06:24.040
And it is the key to your work today.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:25.439
Yes, today it is really the key.

00:06:25.439 --> 00:06:27.560
Back then, it was more about saying:

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:31.079
"Okay, I am at Disneyland in terms of the importance of where I am."

00:06:31.079 --> 00:06:34.720
But I did not feel I was learning that much about creating contacts

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.879
or improving beyond what I could already learn on day one.

00:06:37.879 --> 00:06:41.000
So I made a choice that at the time went a bit against the trend,

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.639
because journalists tended to really want to stay in the newsroom.

00:06:44.639 --> 00:06:48.959
Because it is more comfortable. You work eight hours, you go in, clock in, and that is it,

00:06:48.959 --> 00:06:50.120
you have certainties.

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:55.560
Whereas when you go out, when you ask to be on the street, there are some pros, but also many cons.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:07:00.240
It is hot, it is cold, maybe you wait outside a hotel for a player and they say, "I'll arrive in an hour," then they become three.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:00.959
You have no schedule.

00:07:00.959 --> 00:07:06.680
You do not know when you will go back home. You often go back at night. Maybe you were supposed to finish at 8 p.m., then you finish at night,

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:07.800
so that is part of the life.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:09.000
But that changed my life.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:14.399
That was the real turning point, understanding that I wanted to do that, to be among people.

00:07:14.399 --> 00:07:17.040
And then the streets, here in Milan, were everything for me,

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:20.439
because the streets gave me the chance to meet so many people

00:07:20.439 --> 00:07:23.480
who then followed their own path and became part of my network.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:25.360
Did you have your own show?

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:28.920
How did it work? You waited for players outside hotels and what else?

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:33.240
My role was simply to contribute to the creation of content,

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:36.920
to the development of news throughout the Sky Sport day.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:37.319
Got it.

00:07:37.319 --> 00:07:40.319
So they had their programs, both the continuous ones on Sky Sport 24,

00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:46.360
and the classic evening show with Gianluca Di Marzio and Alessandro Bonan, the classic 11 p.m. transfer market show.

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:48.360
My role was to add content.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:50.199
So what can you find on the street?

00:07:50.199 --> 00:07:55.399
An agent meeting another agent, a club director meeting an agent, a player at lunch,

00:07:55.399 --> 00:07:56.360
a player arriving.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:08:01.120
Very often during the transfer window there was this procedure of "what time does this player arrive?"

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:04.600
Milan, Inter, Juve buy this player, he arrives at Linate at 10,

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:06.399
so you have to find out and go there.

00:08:06.399 --> 00:08:08.360
Sometimes it is secret, sometimes everyone knows.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:11.839
So there was also a very competitive side, I have to admit.

00:08:11.839 --> 00:08:16.959
Were you already focused on transfers, or did that happen gradually during your years at Sky?

00:08:16.959 --> 00:08:20.920
As soon as I got there, I made it clear that for me that could be the only field

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:26.079
where I could truly give my best contribution, because I was never good at other sports.

00:08:26.079 --> 00:08:28.839
The transfer market is something that is now super popular,

00:08:28.839 --> 00:08:33.879
but back then maybe it was fairly niche, because it is almost a business thing.

00:08:33.879 --> 00:08:36.879
Journalistically it was also seen as a problem

00:08:36.879 --> 00:08:40.960
because, as I was saying, it brings many more hours of work than a more technical job

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:41.960
where you analyze, maybe comment...

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:42.840
The game.

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:44.720
...you analyze the game or do commentary.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:48.799
So yes, it definitely had many downsides, but for me, right away, my focus,

00:08:48.799 --> 00:08:52.360
even when I wrote for those sites before joining Sky, was on the transfer market

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:57.600
because there is an adrenaline, a rush, that for me was always too exciting to walk away from

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.519
and so I fell in love with it immediately

00:08:59.519 --> 00:09:04.679
and think that on my first day at Sky they basically asked me to go looking for this player

00:09:04.679 --> 00:09:07.360
who was Mauro Icardi, who had just joined Inter from Sampdoria,

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:10.559
and the only contact I had was actually Icardi's agent.

00:09:10.559 --> 00:09:14.240
For me it was the chance to say, "This is really, really destiny"

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.960
it was the chance to say, "This is the only contact I can count on."

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:19.320
I called this person I knew.

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:24.440
On my first day at Sky, he arrived, and I remember he was at Dolce &amp; Gabbana buying some clothes,

00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:25.759
and I did my first interview with him.

00:09:25.759 --> 00:09:29.600
I went back to the newsroom that first evening saying, "I did an exclusive Icardi interview for Sky."

00:09:29.600 --> 00:09:32.840
At 19 or 20, I was saying, "This is all bigger than me."

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:34.240
You spent a good seven years in there.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:35.320
Yes, I did.

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:36.399
I was there for quite a while.

00:09:36.399 --> 00:09:40.159
While you were there, had you already become, in quotes, known,

00:09:40.159 --> 00:09:43.200
famous in your field, or were you still an underdog?

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.120
I had grown quite a bit, especially on Twitter,

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:48.759
because there was definitely growth, not just in terms of numbers,

00:09:48.759 --> 00:09:50.559
but also in the quality of news about foreign leagues.

00:09:50.559 --> 00:09:56.279
My idea was: I am in a place that is excellent in the transfer market and sports

00:09:56.279 --> 00:10:00.360
as far as Italy is concerned; abroad, the focus was very different

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:04.360
so the focus was 95% Italy and 5% everything else.

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:08.960
I saw this gap, also from what I think is our own Italian cultural point of view.

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:14.639
In football, foreign leagues or even speaking another language are not accepted much,

00:10:14.639 --> 00:10:18.799
even the best sporting directors in Italy often do not even speak English.

00:10:18.799 --> 00:10:24.000
And on top of that, I thought it was a form of respect toward the people around me.

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:28.120
Rather than trying to climb positions inside the company

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:33.279
to overtake those who opened doors for me as a kid, which felt like invading their space.

00:10:33.279 --> 00:10:35.639
If I take the contact of the person sitting next to me

00:10:35.639 --> 00:10:39.159
and they are also the one who believed in me as a kid... that is just not who I am.

00:10:39.159 --> 00:10:41.639
Foreign leagues, instead, were unexplored territory.

00:10:41.639 --> 00:10:45.600
For me it was the chance to say, "Our model works in Italy, why not try it abroad too, by jumping in?"

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:46.399
You were not stepping on anyone's toes.

00:10:46.399 --> 00:10:46.919
Exactly.

00:10:46.919 --> 00:10:50.320
You said, "Okay, since I already do this at Sky, in Italy..."

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:50.840
Yes.

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:53.879
"On my own channels, on Twitter, I can do what I want."

00:10:53.879 --> 00:10:56.200
Yes, I said, "I will try to go all in on other languages."

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:57.639
Did you already speak English?

00:10:57.639 --> 00:10:58.960
So I started writing and tweeting,

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:01.919
posting only in English, and sometimes in Spanish too.

00:11:01.919 --> 00:11:06.200
I never studied English seriously, I never took courses or anything like that,

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:11.120
in fact, those who really know English, can tell I don't speak perfect English, it is more street English.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:13.480
I started because I saw that kind of opportunity, to say:

00:11:13.480 --> 00:11:17.320
"This model works in another language, while bringing our quality",

00:11:17.320 --> 00:11:21.600
and I think Sky played a huge part in that evolution.

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:26.399
Abroad, the big football scoop was tied to many other things that were not always about transfer news.

00:11:26.399 --> 00:11:29.799
There was gossip: in England, the tabloid front pages were all about:

00:11:29.799 --> 00:11:32.200
"That player's wife cheated on that player."

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:33.840
Much less quality transfer news.

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:38.720
Lots of nonsense, lots of fake news that no one checked back then because phones were much less present.

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:42.279
Today there is a higher level of attention, quality and obsession with detail.

00:11:42.279 --> 00:11:46.080
So I said, "Why not try this abroad too, bring this model outside Italy?"

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:48.759
So I started posting only in English

00:11:48.759 --> 00:11:51.960
and above all to understand that there was another key: Instagram.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:56.039
For me, Instagram changed this world: you get in touch with people,

00:11:56.039 --> 00:12:00.720
you can message people, not only players but especially agents, executives,

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:05.039
people in the football world, presenting yourself with a name, a face and saying who knows you.

00:12:05.039 --> 00:12:10.639
Rather than sending a WhatsApp or SMS back then: "Why should I answer you?" "Who are you?"

00:12:10.639 --> 00:12:11.879
When did you open your Instagram account?

00:12:11.879 --> 00:12:16.120
I opened my account very early, but it was like everyone's Instagram back then:

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:19.799
girlfriend, friends, relatives, sunsets, lunches and dinners.

00:12:19.799 --> 00:12:22.000
But then, while I was growing a lot on Twitter,

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:26.039
many people on Instagram came looking for me, expecting transfer news there too,

00:12:26.039 --> 00:12:31.480
and many commented, "We do not care", "Where is Godin going?", "Where is Messi going?", "Where is Icardi going?"

00:12:31.480 --> 00:12:34.879
And that was when I said, "Okay, I need to change this."

00:12:34.879 --> 00:12:38.240
"From today, I will use Instagram only for work too."

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:43.879
To recap, Fabrizio starts from zero, knocks on every door and even offers to write for free just to begin.

00:12:43.879 --> 00:12:48.360
Step by step he reaches the Sky newsroom, and while working there he notices something very important:

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:50.120
there is a huge gap in the market.

00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:55.919
No one was covering transfer news in English yet, on social media, also talking about international teams.

00:12:55.919 --> 00:12:58.440
And so he does it, with internet timing and language,

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:01.960
quickly becoming extremely famous on Twitter and then Instagram.

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:04.919
In a sense, you are not unlike a Bloomberg journalist,

00:13:04.919 --> 00:13:10.279
except instead of talking with company managers who give them information before it is public

00:13:10.279 --> 00:13:15.080
so they can publish it first on Bloomberg, you do it with agents, managers, relatives,

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:16.200
with players' friends.

00:13:16.200 --> 00:13:22.480
Having a group of people who trust you, and whom you trust, from whom you can get news,

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:24.320
is a huge part of your job.

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:27.399
How did you build this network and what was your ABC?

00:13:27.399 --> 00:13:29.399
Those are the two paths.

00:13:29.399 --> 00:13:32.080
The first is the traditional one, which for me remains fundamental.

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:36.879
The streets still introduced me to about 20 or 30% of the contacts I have today.

00:13:36.879 --> 00:13:42.840
Then of course Instagram was the other path, helping you build even more network, to have many more contacts,

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:44.559
and make it international.

00:13:44.559 --> 00:13:46.759
But for me, the streets started everything.

00:13:46.759 --> 00:13:49.679
I spent my days outside hotels, outside restaurants,

00:13:49.679 --> 00:13:53.279
outside anywhere I knew I could meet or run into someone,

00:13:53.279 --> 00:13:57.440
not only to get content for Sky, but also to create my own contact.

00:13:57.440 --> 00:14:01.320
It happened to me to meet people and have news

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:06.559
that could have been important at that moment, but I chose to sacrifice it in exchange for a relationship.

00:14:06.559 --> 00:14:10.519
And say, "Look, today this meeting never happened, because I know it could create a problem,

00:14:10.519 --> 00:14:15.080
but let me know at the right time, and you will remember how I behaved."

00:14:15.080 --> 00:14:18.200
Rather than always being that journalist seen in a negative way.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:22.320
My goal was always to bring a positive feeling around the figure of the journalist,

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:24.240
who has always been an enemy for many people.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:29.799
In the long run it pays off. You sacrifice short term scoops, instead of being Fabrizio Romano,

00:14:29.799 --> 00:14:31.759
otherwise you would have been Fabrizio Corona.

00:14:31.759 --> 00:14:34.720
In fact, Fabrizio's case is truly one of a kind.

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:39.919
The 2000s built the image of the journalist as a predator, always hunting for news.

00:14:39.919 --> 00:14:41.159
But Fabrizio did the opposite.

00:14:41.159 --> 00:14:44.519
Instead of treating his sources like prey, he built real relationships with them.

00:14:44.519 --> 00:14:48.159
And even at the cost of missing some scoops, he prioritized his reputation,

00:14:48.159 --> 00:14:51.000
which over the years allowed him to have the largest network of all.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:55.360
It is a bit like Tommaso Ebhardt, whom I already interviewed, who after years chasing Sergio Marchionne,

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:58.480
managed to earn his trust, to the point of writing his biography.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:03.039
For me it was always a different idea, in terms of the kind of relationship.

00:15:03.039 --> 00:15:05.399
In fact, my biggest problem today is exactly this:

00:15:05.399 --> 00:15:09.039
you risk losing a bit of that daily fighting spirit.

00:15:09.039 --> 00:15:11.879
To do this job well, how many contacts should a person have?

00:15:11.879 --> 00:15:13.320
In my opinion, as many as possible.

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:16.759
I am probably crazy for wanting to do this job internationally.

00:15:16.759 --> 00:15:20.759
So it means that on the same day you talk with a River Plate executive in Buenos Aires,

00:15:20.759 --> 00:15:23.080
with someone from Arsenal in London, with someone from Paris Saint Germain.

00:15:23.080 --> 00:15:25.440
Really, anyone can come in.

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:28.759
Do you have a system to manage contacts, or do you do everything by instinct?

00:15:28.759 --> 00:15:29.679
No. I tried.

00:15:29.679 --> 00:15:33.919
At times I tried to have a map, to say, "Okay, let's try to organize this,

00:15:33.919 --> 00:15:35.320
because otherwise you lose something."

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:37.120
But then I understood this is the only way.

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:43.440
On Christmas Day, before dinner, in Naples, between 4 and 7 p.m., I try to call as many as possible,

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:44.679
I basically go around the world.

00:15:44.679 --> 00:15:48.840
I say: "Okay, Portugal, Sporting, Porto, Benfica. I call this one, this one and this one.

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.200
Agents: this one, this one, this one."

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:54.120
And I try to call everyone or at least write to everyone, to keep a solid contact.

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.399
How much of your day do you spend on the phone?

00:15:56.399 --> 00:15:59.320
All of it. Apart from exceptions, I am basically always on the phone.

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:01.320
And how many are targeted calls?

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:04.440
And how much is simply exploring and looking for opportunities?

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:05.480
It depends on the period.

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:09.679
During the transfer window, it is different from a period like now,

00:16:09.679 --> 00:16:11.480
which is more of a preparation phase.

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:16.399
Many targeted calls, either about news you received or to check something already circulating,

00:16:16.399 --> 00:16:17.840
to understand whether it is true or not.

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:20.440
But sometimes there is also "trying to understand."

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:23.440
For me the most important thing is using your instinct.

00:16:23.440 --> 00:16:24.559
Using your own head.

00:16:24.559 --> 00:16:26.200
I say that "the transfer market is like a puzzle."

00:16:26.200 --> 00:16:30.039
You start in late May, early June, with all the pieces thrown on the table.

00:16:30.039 --> 00:16:31.919
And you also have to add something of your own.

00:16:31.919 --> 00:16:33.759
What you are saying has to make sense.

00:16:33.759 --> 00:16:36.840
You already sense many things that could happen.

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:39.759
You already know all the people, what situation they are in.

00:16:39.759 --> 00:16:40.519
You can understand them.

00:16:40.519 --> 00:16:45.960
Then if you watch many games and follow football a lot, you know what that team or another might need.

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:46.960
You know the relationships.

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:50.600
The hardest thing at this level is knowing the relationships.

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:52.320
In the end, they are always people.

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:56.159
Sometimes people imagine football as a world where aliens do things among themselves.

00:16:56.159 --> 00:16:58.480
But they are people, and relationships make the difference.

00:16:58.480 --> 00:17:01.360
And those relationships can often lead you to sense a story.

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:04.400
I know that club is looking for a striker.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:05.640
Who could the striker be?

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:10.000
But if you know the relationship with the club, the agent and the kind of player,

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:12.960
if you put the pieces together, you do not always get it right,

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:16.400
but sometimes you make a call and say, "I thought of this, could it be?"

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:17.359
And it has happened to me.

00:17:17.359 --> 00:17:21.759
You have like 40 million followers on Instagram alone, or something like that.

00:17:21.759 --> 00:17:26.880
When a traditional, digital creator has those numbers, it is rarely just him, right?

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:28.920
Usually it is a media company.

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:29.240
That's true.

00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:33.519
Maybe he is the face, a bit like Mr. Beast, and then there are hundreds of people inside.

00:17:33.519 --> 00:17:35.359
It seems to me that you are pretty lean instead.

00:17:35.359 --> 00:17:37.839
Yes, I do 90% of things by myself.

00:17:37.839 --> 00:17:43.000
Everything I post on Instagram and Twitter is always posted only by me.

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:44.960
So no one else enters my account.

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:49.160
Then I have two people who help me with graphics and video editing,

00:17:49.160 --> 00:17:51.640
things I partly have no time for and partly no skill for.

00:17:51.640 --> 00:17:55.720
So I have two people I can tell, "Make this player in a Milan jersey for me."

00:17:55.720 --> 00:17:57.880
And I have the graphic ready within ten minutes.

00:17:57.880 --> 00:18:01.880
And there is one person, one of my best friends, named Matteo Moretto,

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:04.880
who works with me on the news side, so the more editorial part.

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:08.079
He has many contacts, a bit different too, but above all he is my friend.

00:18:08.079 --> 00:18:09.480
For me, that made the difference.

00:18:09.480 --> 00:18:09.880
Well, sure.

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:14.519
At this pace, surrounding myself with people who are never my employees, but above all my friends.

00:18:14.519 --> 00:18:17.640
With artificial intelligence, I think you can speed up your work a lot.

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:20.880
It has helped me a lot, especially with this part, graphics.

00:18:20.880 --> 00:18:25.519
As for content or text processing, I have never used it in my life.

00:18:25.519 --> 00:18:30.079
I would never have artificial intelligence write a text, because it is my classic approach,

00:18:30.079 --> 00:18:32.359
journalistically unacceptable as I see it.

00:18:32.359 --> 00:18:35.920
Graphically, it helps you be very fast.

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:42.640
And then there is another issue, not always easy to understand: you do not just buy a player and that is it,

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:47.240
there are renegotiation rights, buybacks, you buy a percentage.

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:49.680
And those are boring things to explain, right?

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:51.920
So the skill is not only breaking the news,

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:55.839
but I imagine also telling it as clearly, directly and quickly as possible.

00:18:55.839 --> 00:19:00.160
Absolutely, and especially understanding the difference between the platforms where you tell it.

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:04.079
Social media has often been treated as a support tool,

00:19:04.079 --> 00:19:06.640
but never as something to truly focus on.

00:19:06.640 --> 00:19:11.680
Instead, for me the fun is exactly saying: "Today I know Messi is going to Barcelona."

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:13.160
Twitter is breaking news.

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:14.920
On Instagram, the graphic has to be perfect.

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:18.519
I care a lot about the text: the bold parts and everything else, the right emojis.

00:19:18.519 --> 00:19:21.160
Then there is TikTok, which is another way of telling it.

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:23.799
On YouTube I take ten minutes to explain to people:

00:19:23.799 --> 00:19:28.880
"Messi is going to Barcelona, but why, how, how much he will earn, what that contract clause means."

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:34.440
There are many different ways to tell the same thing on many platforms, reaching different people.

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:36.240
And that is something I really enjoy.

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:40.160
For me, output quality and aesthetics are crucial today.

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:44.240
Looking at your accounts, with the professional bias of having an agency...

00:19:44.240 --> 00:19:45.000
Sure.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:46.839
...I see you are not a typical creator.

00:19:46.839 --> 00:19:50.720
You post a lot, very specifically, you are a machine, always football.

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:55.000
So I struggle to understand how you monetize this activity.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:57.359
How do you manage to sustain it?

00:19:57.359 --> 00:20:02.880
Through sponsors: my business model has always been tied to never wanting anyone to pay,

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:07.319
on my platforms no one will ever say, "I spent one euro to read a Fabrizio story."

00:20:07.319 --> 00:20:11.720
So I always said, "I want to be fast, I want finding my content to be truly easy."

00:20:11.720 --> 00:20:16.160
Even one extra click, like saying, "Come to my site to read," costs in today's world,

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:19.000
not economically, but in terms of user effort.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:23.720
Even though many times people proposed, "Why do we not make a Fabrizio Romano Here We Go app?"

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:27.759
"Pay one euro a month, with your numbers think how much money you could make."

00:20:27.759 --> 00:20:31.200
Yes, but several different factors led me to this decision.

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:35.720
First of all my approach, but also being able to say that no one will ever,

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:41.119
in quotes, blame me for things that do not go the right way and do not depend on me.

00:20:41.119 --> 00:20:45.519
Because in the end I am not a creator who decides the outcome of what he is telling you.

00:20:45.519 --> 00:20:49.759
Today I can tell you that Milan is buying that player, maybe the medicals are already booked,

00:20:49.759 --> 00:20:51.759
everything is ready, and tomorrow morning his wife wakes up

00:20:51.759 --> 00:20:54.519
and says, "I no longer want to live in Milan, let us go somewhere else."

00:20:54.519 --> 00:20:58.880
And then people can say, "I even paid for this service and you are betraying my trust."

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:00.160
How do you work with sponsors?

00:21:00.160 --> 00:21:02.000
There can be many ways to collaborate.

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:08.359
The more traditional ones: video spots or similar things. It happened with Pepsi or other brands.

00:21:08.359 --> 00:21:11.880
Or, which is what I prefer, integrating them into my content.

00:21:11.880 --> 00:21:16.079
Meaning giving the brand visibility inside what I am already doing.

00:21:16.079 --> 00:21:18.839
For example, there can be branded live streams,

00:21:18.839 --> 00:21:23.039
the chance for users to ask questions, something that happens very little on my channels.

00:21:23.039 --> 00:21:29.240
Or integrating them into my announcements: I remember doing a collaboration with Turkish Airlines.

00:21:29.240 --> 00:21:33.680
Every time I said "Here We Go," the brand appeared with the airplane drawing

00:21:33.680 --> 00:21:35.720
simulating the departure or transfer.

00:21:35.720 --> 00:21:40.559
I love this part, I enjoy the chance to integrate the brand in the right way

00:21:40.559 --> 00:21:43.039
so it is not perceived as the classic paid plug.

00:21:43.039 --> 00:21:44.920
In fact, when you say, "I do not understand how you monetize,"

00:21:44.920 --> 00:21:46.599
for me it is the biggest compliment possible.

00:21:46.599 --> 00:21:47.240
It is perfect.

00:21:47.240 --> 00:21:49.640
Clearly, life is not bad once you reach success:

00:21:49.640 --> 00:21:54.680
dinners with famous football players, doing your dream job, lots of attention, tons of followers,

00:21:54.680 --> 00:21:57.759
but when you are in the spotlight, things do not always go as they should.

00:21:57.759 --> 00:22:00.680
What was the hardest moment in this journey?

00:22:00.680 --> 00:22:05.119
I think it is defending yourself when you are attacked for something you know you did not do.

00:22:05.119 --> 00:22:10.000
I will always remember something an important president told me when I was attacked once.

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:11.519
He told me, "Do not bring them into your ring."

00:22:11.519 --> 00:22:14.319
When you have a big following, it is natural to want to defend yourself,

00:22:14.319 --> 00:22:18.200
because if I did not do something, or I want to explain what happened while people attack you,

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:23.359
you want to reply, but you do not, because among so many people maybe 10% or 20% saw it.

00:22:23.359 --> 00:22:27.359
So why show the whole 80% what happened and plant doubt even in people

00:22:27.359 --> 00:22:29.359
who maybe had no idea what was happening?

00:22:29.359 --> 00:22:32.400
But something happened to me in Denmark a couple of years ago.

00:22:32.400 --> 00:22:35.160
A newspaper wrote an article saying:

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:39.720
"Fabrizio Romano gets paid, and if someone wants to post about their players, promote them,

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:46.799
they can pay 5,000 kroner to have advertising for their players on his accounts."

00:22:46.799 --> 00:22:48.440
I said, "Excuse me, where did you get this story?"

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:52.680
They said, "No, no, we are sure of what we are saying because we saw emails from this person

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:55.359
who offered these services to clubs for this possibility."

00:22:55.359 --> 00:22:56.880
I said, "Yes, but I do not know this person.

00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:01.200
If you can prove I have contact with this person, an email, WhatsApp, phone call."

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:02.200
It makes no sense.

00:23:02.200 --> 00:23:07.000
I said, "As journalists, since you point the finger, you must first verify the kind of relationship."

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:09.960
And so they claimed there is this app, called Cameo,

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:14.759
where you can get videos from people, happy birthday messages or things like that,

00:23:14.759 --> 00:23:18.000
and they claimed these services could be obtained through that app.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:18.920
It never happened!

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:22.720
But the problem is that the article comes out, Norway picks it up, then Portugal picks it up,

00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:24.039
then England picks it up.

00:23:24.039 --> 00:23:26.359
And the idea becomes, "You get paid to post."

00:23:26.359 --> 00:23:28.720
In these situations you feel like saying, "Now I will destroy you."

00:23:28.720 --> 00:23:33.079
But on the other hand you say, "Why should I create even more confusion?"

00:23:33.079 --> 00:23:35.559
Still, for me the difficult moment was exactly saying:

00:23:35.559 --> 00:23:38.480
"It is awful to be attacked without doing anything and not be able to respond."

00:23:38.480 --> 00:23:43.160
Except legally, but I only spent money to defend myself legally,

00:23:43.160 --> 00:23:46.079
to get a statement from the Danish journalists' association

00:23:46.079 --> 00:23:49.519
forcing the site to publish an article denying what they had reported,

00:23:49.519 --> 00:23:51.960
but of course no one paid any attention to that part.

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:55.359
I think by now there has been a good process of educating fans.

00:23:55.359 --> 00:24:00.880
At the beginning there was this feeling of saying, "You got it wrong, you reported false things!"

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:05.119
In the long run, people have learned to respect the fact

00:24:05.119 --> 00:24:09.279
that neither I nor we journalists control negotiations.

00:24:09.279 --> 00:24:11.319
So the important thing is always to tell the truth.

00:24:11.319 --> 00:24:16.359
Clearly, if today I come to you and say, "Messi is going to Juventus," I am telling you a false story.

00:24:16.359 --> 00:24:21.359
But if I tell you, "Messi has an agreement with that club and then tomorrow morning changes his mind,"

00:24:21.359 --> 00:24:23.440
he will probably be the one to tell you it was true.

00:24:23.440 --> 00:24:26.440
I think now there is much more education from this point of view.

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:29.319
With Messi many said, "You reported something false,"

00:24:29.319 --> 00:24:33.799
because I had said Messi already had an agreement ready to sign with Barcelona to renew and stay there,

00:24:33.799 --> 00:24:35.920
but then he ended up in Paris.

00:24:35.920 --> 00:24:39.480
That day many people told me, "But you said he would renew, it was all done."

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:43.319
Then luckily Messi himself said, "I was there with my family to sign the contract,

00:24:43.319 --> 00:24:47.400
the contracts were ready, but Barcelona told me that for financial fair play reasons

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:49.359
and economic reasons, we can no longer proceed."

00:24:49.359 --> 00:24:54.599
Today players and clubs communicate much more on social media than before.

00:24:54.599 --> 00:24:57.599
Yes, you take a risk, much more risk than in the past.

00:24:57.599 --> 00:25:00.960
In the past, when I was a kid, you bought a newspaper,

00:25:00.960 --> 00:25:05.839
and that newspaper or TV show news had no immediate response.

00:25:05.839 --> 00:25:11.240
Today, if I write on Instagram that a player is joining a team and it is completely false,

00:25:11.240 --> 00:25:15.880
it takes five seconds to comment below, make a story and say, "This guy is making this up."

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:20.720
So I think this is positive, also to avoid fake news spreading around.

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:23.119
And especially for young people who want to do this job,

00:25:23.119 --> 00:25:26.880
they need to understand that the quality of your work always comes first.

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:28.160
Does being first matter that much?

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:30.640
I think it matters a lot, especially when you want to become known.

00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:32.960
Having information before others is extremely important.

00:25:32.960 --> 00:25:36.440
In the long run, though, accuracy matters a lot to me.

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:39.200
I mean, the quality of the information becomes truly fundamental.

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:41.599
How much time passes after a call,

00:25:41.599 --> 00:25:44.559
from when you are fairly sure of information to when you can publish it?

00:25:44.559 --> 00:25:48.480
Sometimes I have sources I trust completely and I publish after five seconds,

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:49.960
maybe while I am still on the phone.

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:51.680
Because I fear they may call someone else.

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:54.000
So they call me and say, "...we are signing this player for 22 million."

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:54.880
And you are there, "Here We Go."

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:59.039
I am already on the phone, putting it on speaker and writing, then I publish as soon as I hang up.

00:25:59.039 --> 00:26:01.079
Other times I trust it very little

00:26:01.079 --> 00:26:06.359
because maybe it came from someone I do not have such deep contacts with.

00:26:06.359 --> 00:26:09.599
Then you need a verification process that can take a long time.

00:26:09.599 --> 00:26:13.759
I remember Modrić to Milan last summer, which was one of the most satisfying stories for me.

00:26:13.759 --> 00:26:15.920
I worked on it for 24 hours.

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:17.200
I knew it the night before.

00:26:17.200 --> 00:26:19.599
You live with that anxiety that someone else might say it.

00:26:19.599 --> 00:26:24.960
But it is so big, so delicate also in terms of relationships, that you need extra steps.

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:26.920
You compete more with newspapers, right?

00:26:26.920 --> 00:26:29.400
There are few of us doing this job this way.

00:26:29.400 --> 00:26:33.240
There are one or two very good transfer journalists per country.

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:37.680
I am starting to forgive myself a bit, while in the early years I wanted to be first on everything, over everyone,

00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:39.519
because I had that ambition.

00:26:39.519 --> 00:26:43.160
Today I understand that I think I am the only one doing it internationally.

00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:47.599
Was there a moment when you grew fastest overall, or was it steady year after year?

00:26:47.599 --> 00:26:53.079
The only thing I remember well is that in July 2020 I was celebrating a friend's birthday

00:26:53.079 --> 00:26:56.400
and I had 433,000 followers on Instagram.

00:26:56.400 --> 00:26:58.119
It was July 15, 2020.

00:26:58.119 --> 00:27:02.680
I remember this because I work with an Instagram page called "433",

00:27:02.680 --> 00:27:07.640
and they had made me a graphic because I had matched "433", which is their number.

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:09.000
So I remember that moment.

00:27:09.000 --> 00:27:11.599
But that is my only memory of numbers on my account.

00:27:11.599 --> 00:27:13.640
So you have grown a hundredfold since Covid.

00:27:13.640 --> 00:27:18.000
Yes, between 2021 and 2022 came the biggest explosion,

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:20.599
because the way people communicate really changed.

00:27:20.599 --> 00:27:25.599
I remember that when I started posting transfer news on Instagram, Corriere did not have an account.

00:27:25.599 --> 00:27:31.799
Instagram was very limited for information and focused on lifestyle, not breaking news.

00:27:31.799 --> 00:27:37.359
Do you know which story you broke that gave you the biggest boost?

00:27:37.359 --> 00:27:41.720
Look, in terms of changing my path, it was a story

00:27:41.720 --> 00:27:46.079
about Bruno Fernandes to Manchester United, because that story was fundamental for me

00:27:46.079 --> 00:27:48.599
to become known definitively at an international level.

00:27:48.599 --> 00:27:51.720
United, probably together with Real Madrid and Barcelona, are the three teams

00:27:51.720 --> 00:27:53.319
with the biggest following in the world.

00:27:53.319 --> 00:27:56.119
It looked like the deal would no longer happen, it was supposed to happen the previous summer,

00:27:56.119 --> 00:27:56.839
and I had said no.

00:27:56.839 --> 00:27:59.039
Everyone said, "Why do you say no? Everyone says yes."

00:27:59.039 --> 00:28:02.440
A fan prefers beautiful false news to ugly true news.

00:28:02.440 --> 00:28:05.839
So I was seen a bit as the bad guy saying it would not happen that summer.

00:28:05.839 --> 00:28:08.000
In January I said, "Now yes, now it happens."

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:12.279
I posted the photo of Bruno Fernandes on a plane with his agent, and everyone went crazy

00:28:12.279 --> 00:28:15.599
saying, okay, this guy really knows the people inside out.

00:28:15.599 --> 00:28:17.400
So that was a decisive moment for me.

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:22.559
Then, if you ask me in terms of numbers, it was probably Cristiano Ronaldo's return to Manchester United.

00:28:22.559 --> 00:28:27.359
I remember I was live on Twitch and there were 97,000 people connected,

00:28:27.359 --> 00:28:30.000
more than at Camp Nou, and I was saying, "Just to hear me say three words."

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:34.640
That was an epic moment because, you know, put Cristiano and Manchester United together... devastating.

00:28:34.640 --> 00:28:39.720
What advantage does a club, a player or an agent have in giving that kind of news?

00:28:39.720 --> 00:28:41.279
It is really a matter of friendship.

00:28:41.279 --> 00:28:45.799
Very often a relationship forms where today you help me, but tomorrow I can help you.

00:28:45.799 --> 00:28:50.000
That does not mean promoting players, it can simply mean exchanging information.

00:28:50.000 --> 00:28:53.839
Many agents know that I talk, more or less, with many people every day

00:28:53.839 --> 00:28:58.519
and it can become an opportunity to say, "You know, I am trying to do this deal with this club."

00:28:58.720 --> 00:29:03.079
And I might think, "Look, I heard Napoli is looking for that type of player."

00:29:03.079 --> 00:29:06.759
You can be the go-to person that helps everyone get one more idea.

00:29:06.759 --> 00:29:07.200
Yes.

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:09.039
This can be one way to do this job

00:29:09.039 --> 00:29:12.480
that many times later helped me gain access to many more people.

00:29:12.480 --> 00:29:13.680
The other thing is the numbers.

00:29:13.680 --> 00:29:17.480
I mean, today, if you give news to a newspaper, it is probably seen in that country

00:29:17.480 --> 00:29:21.559
by people no younger than 30 or 35.

00:29:21.559 --> 00:29:22.480
You speak to everyone.

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:24.359
On my account, kids watch it, my dad watches it.

00:29:24.359 --> 00:29:26.680
For the future, how long do you see yourself doing this?

00:29:26.680 --> 00:29:30.079
And I imagine it is also a market that will get more and more saturated,

00:29:30.079 --> 00:29:33.599
are there evolutions you want to pursue, or do you think it will still last long?

00:29:33.599 --> 00:29:36.640
No, actually I think it will be very short in this form.

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:42.200
My idea is to start making a change very soon

00:29:42.200 --> 00:29:44.640
and I think the World Cup is an opportunity in that sense.

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:50.359
Everyone's attention, in the middle of the market, in mid summer, June 15 to July 20,

00:29:50.359 --> 00:29:54.039
will all be on the World Cup, so transfer news will matter much less

00:29:54.039 --> 00:29:57.119
and injuries, who plays, who does not, who advances, who does not, will matter much more.

00:29:57.119 --> 00:30:01.839
And above all, on my side, physically, I will have to be on the other side of the world

00:30:01.839 --> 00:30:03.440
so there will be time differences.

00:30:03.440 --> 00:30:08.839
So what I want to do is limit much more the follow up of a story,

00:30:08.839 --> 00:30:13.799
what made me explode over the years was also carrying a story to the finish line.

00:30:13.799 --> 00:30:16.640
It is not only telling you, "Milan wants to buy that striker,"

00:30:16.640 --> 00:30:19.599
but also saying, "Milan wants that player," and I tell you today,

00:30:19.599 --> 00:30:21.079
tomorrow I tell you, "They offered 10 million,"

00:30:21.079 --> 00:30:25.359
the next day, "They want 12," then, "They want 17." There is a path to the story.

00:30:25.359 --> 00:30:28.319
Unfortunately, it does not always all happen in a few hours.

00:30:28.319 --> 00:30:29.359
That would be much easier.

00:30:29.359 --> 00:30:32.799
Often you have to stay in that process, sometimes exhausting, lasting weeks,

00:30:32.799 --> 00:30:34.759
saying, "They want more," "the offer comes Monday"...

00:30:34.759 --> 00:30:36.200
And you keep it all in your head, no process?

00:30:36.200 --> 00:30:37.000
No, nothing.

00:30:37.000 --> 00:30:40.160
The point is that all this process, I think, has tired me a bit

00:30:40.160 --> 00:30:42.519
and I believe it is starting to tire people a bit too,

00:30:42.519 --> 00:30:45.279
so my idea is: okay, breaking news, I want to keep that,

00:30:45.279 --> 00:30:49.079
okay, this "Here We Go" magic of saying, "The deal is done and finished,"

00:30:49.079 --> 00:30:54.119
but let us say I want to tell the beginning and the end, and leave the rest a bit freer.

00:30:54.119 --> 00:30:57.200
After the World Cup, I want to invest much more in quality.

00:30:57.200 --> 00:31:00.799
I want to do more interviews, more quality content

00:31:00.799 --> 00:31:03.480
with all the contacts I have gathered over the years

00:31:03.480 --> 00:31:08.599
to free myself from the problem I mentioned earlier, that today breaking news is often a problem for me.

00:31:08.599 --> 00:31:11.240
I would bother someone and create a problem for someone.

00:31:11.240 --> 00:31:15.000
Having so many friends involved, maybe the time has come to arrive a little later,

00:31:15.000 --> 00:31:18.279
keeping more of a quality filter on the news I try to guarantee people.

00:31:18.279 --> 00:31:22.680
So not just telling you who Napoli, Milan, Inter or Juve will buy,

00:31:22.680 --> 00:31:26.960
but maybe also why, interviewing the sporting director, the player, the agent.

00:31:26.960 --> 00:31:28.240
Yes, a full media company.

00:31:28.240 --> 00:31:28.519
Yes.

00:31:28.519 --> 00:31:32.240
I also have this desire to have more room to tell stories,

00:31:32.240 --> 00:31:35.119
not just be under the constant pressure of news.

00:31:35.119 --> 00:31:39.400
Today you can break the best news in the world, but tomorrow morning people have already forgotten it,

00:31:39.400 --> 00:31:40.359
and they already want another one.

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:41.200
That was great!

00:31:41.200 --> 00:31:41.920
Thank you so much.

00:31:41.920 --> 00:31:42.720
Thank you.

00:31:42.720 --> 00:31:47.680
With his story, Fabrizio managed to make even me passionate about transfers, though I know nothing about football!

00:31:47.680 --> 00:31:49.799
His success is a mix of many factors.

00:31:49.799 --> 00:31:53.039
Doing first on social media something no one had ever done there,

00:31:53.039 --> 00:31:55.720
doing it internationally where everyone else did it locally,

00:31:55.720 --> 00:31:59.359
and prioritizing reputation and respect with a long term vision.

00:31:59.359 --> 00:32:03.799
Let me know what you think, whether you knew Fabrizio Romano and what you thought of this video.

00:32:03.799 --> 00:32:07.240
Also write in the comments which other people you would like to see on this channel!

